17:12:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: # Not Necessarily—Even Blockbusters Get Burned You'd think a big budget buys permanent security, but that's often not how music licensing works, even for major films. ## Why Money Doesn't Guarantee "Forever" Rights **Rights holders often refuse perpetual deals on principle.** Some artists/estates (or their labels) prefer to negotiate in chunks—they want leverage to renegotiate later, especially if the film becomes a hit and they feel they left money on the table. **Studios sometimes intentionally limit scope to save money.** Paying for "all media, all territories, forever" is exponentially more expensive than paying for "theatrical + domestic DVD for 15 years." Studios often gamble on narrower deals, thinking they'll deal with expansion later if needed. **New technology didn't exist yet.** A deal signed in 1995 literally couldn't include "streaming rights" because streaming as we know it didn't exist. No contract, however well-funded, can secure rights to a distribution method not yet invented. ## Real Blockbuster Examples **Space Jam (1996)** — This is probably the most famous case. Despite being a massive hit with a huge soundtrack (including "I Believe I Can Fly"), the film was **absent from HBO Max at launch** in 2020 because music rights for streaming hadn't been secured. It took additional negotiation before it could be added. **Dazed and Confused (1993)** — Famous for its wall-to-wall needle-drop soundtrack. Licensing costs were so extreme that they blew a huge chunk of the budget, and there have been persistent rumors about difficulty maintaining full music rights across all later formats. **Forrest Gump** — Even though Paramount spent enormous sums locking down its soundtrack, that doesn't automatically extend to *every* future platform or territory that didn't exist at the time. ## The Bigger Picture Big budgets can **reduce** the odds of this happening (since studios can afford broader deals), but they don't **eliminate** it. Rights holders know their leverage increases as a film's popularity grows, so some intentionally hold back full/perpetual rights, betting they can extract more money down the line—especially from a successful blockbuster.
