17:12:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: # It's Not Really "Forgetting"—It's a Structural Problem Great question. The short answer: **it's not incompetence, it's math and negotiation complexity.** ## The Multi-Party Problem Space Jam's soundtrack wasn't just one song—it had contributions from **multiple different artists, labels, and publishers** (R. Kelly, Seal, Coolio, etc.), each with their own rights holders, contracts, and terms. To secure "future-proof" streaming rights back in 1996, Warner Bros. would have needed: - Every single artist/label to agree - To predict and pre-negotiate for a technology (subscription streaming) that **didn't exist yet** - To pay for a right that had no defined value at the time (how do you price "unlimited streaming" in 1996?) ## Nobody "Forgets"—They Prioritize With a studio's entire back catalog, relicensing older titles for new formats is a **massive, ongoing administrative task**. It's not that WB forgot Space Jam specifically—it's that: 1. **Streaming rights weren't part of the original 1996 deal** because streaming wasn't a category yet. 2. When HBO Max launched, WB had to go through their **entire library** and negotiate/renegotiate deals for thousands of titles simultaneously. 3. Films with heavy, multi-artist soundtracks (like Space Jam) are **exponentially harder** to clear because you need agreement from every rights holder, not just one. 4. Some rights holders may **hold out for better terms**, knowing the studio wants their title on the platform. ## The Business Calculus Studios also make **cost-benefit decisions**. If clearing music for one film costs more in negotiation time/money than the film is expected to earn on the platform, it gets deprioritized—even if it's a beloved title. ## The Resolution Space Jam *did* eventually make it to HBO Max, but it took time because Warner Bros. had to go back and strike new deals with the relevant parties for the streaming right specifically—something the original 1996 contracts never anticipated because streaming didn't exist. **Bottom line:** It's less "the studio dropped the ball" and more "you can't sign a contract for a technology that hasn't been invented yet, and untangling multi-artist soundtracks after the fact is genuinely hard."
