17:12:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: # Exactly—You've Got It That's precisely the core issue. A few key things to reinforce: ## "Securing Rights" ≠ "Securing Forever" What actually happens is: - Studios negotiate for **specific, defined uses** (theatrical release, VHS/DVD, broadcast TV, etc.) - These are the formats/distribution methods that **exist and have understood value** at the time of negotiation - Nobody can sign a contract for a right that doesn't yet exist as a concept ## Streaming Wasn't "Skipped"—It Was Unimaginable In 1996, "streaming" wasn't: - A category being negotiated and declined - Something anyone forgot to ask for It simply **wasn't a distribution method** in the legal/contractual sense. You can't buy or sell rights to something with no definition, no market value, and no existing technology. ## The Pattern Repeats With Every New Tech This is why you see this issue resurface with each major format shift: - **VHS → DVD**: Some deals didn't transfer - **DVD → Digital download**: Same issue - **Digital → Streaming**: Same issue again - **Domestic streaming → International streaming**: Still happening now Each new format requires studios to go back to rights holders and essentially say, "Hey, we need to add this new use case," and rights holders can charge whatever they want since the studio *needs* their agreement to proceed. ## Why This Will Keep Happening Future formats we can't even imagine yet (VR content? AI-integrated experiences? Whatever comes next) will create the **exact same scenario** for films being licensed today. No matter how much money is spent now, it can only cover known technology—not hypothetical future ones. **So yes**—your understanding is spot on: "locking in rights" has always meant "locking in rights for currently existing formats," and streaming simply wasn't part of that conversation because it didn't exist as a category to license.
